            <ArticleTitle>Discovery of N-(3-(morpholinomethyl)-phenyl)-amides as potent and selective CB2 agonists.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>5004-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.bmcl.2009.07.057</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recently sulfamoyl benzamides were identified as a novel series of cannabinoid receptor ligands. Replacing the sulfonamide functionality and reversing the original carboxamide bond led to the discovery of N-(3-(morpholinomethyl)-phenyl)-amides as potent and selective CB(2) agonists. Selective CB(2) agonist 31 (K(i)=2.7; CB(1)/CB(2)=190) displayed robust activity in a rodent model of postoperative pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
